# Fixed 2026-09-30, defect 131's class, lane recovery-b4, beside
# `fixedbugs-131-a-sigil-before-a-name-is-one-message`. A sigil before a name
# that writes no value into it is one message too, where it cost two or
# three, and no fix of it is certain. Before a declaration whose type `=`
# follows, `v: i64 @= 0` and `v: i64 = 0` are as likely, so deleting the `@`
# is a guess; alone, before a call, which is no place, or before a
# comparison, nothing is offered: what the line computes is the checker's to
# judge once the sigil is gone. The rest of the line is read, so Ruby's `+=`
# is told at its `=`, as it is with no sigil before it.

function a_declaration()
    @v: i64 = 0  #~ expected_expression
    print(v)

function alone()
    v: i64 @= 0
    @v  #~ expected_expression
    print(v)

function before_an_operator_and_equals()
    v: i64 @= 0
    @v += 1  #~ expected_expression expected_expression
    print(v)

function before_a_call()
    @f(x: 1) = 2  #~ expected_expression

function before_a_comparison()
    v: i64 @= 0
    @v == 1  #~ expected_expression
    print(v)

function f(x: i64) -> i64
    return x
